  {
    "title": "Human Factors and Human–Machine Work Design",
    "cat": "Integrated Production",
    "body": [
      "Automation changes workers' cognitive load, posture, work rhythm, and responsibilities. An unclear interface or excessive alarms can cause operators to ignore warnings.",
      "Design loading and unloading points, working height, reach distance, displays, sound signals, and reset actions around actual users. Involve operators and maintenance staff during the design stage.",
      "Assess ergonomic risk, operating errors, training time, and user acceptance. Do not use cameras or individual productivity data beyond transparent purposes and applicable rules.",
      "**Business analysis and scope.** For “Human Factors and Human–Machine Work Design,” establish the current process before investing: inputs, outputs, product variants, operator tasks, cycle time, changeover time, defects, downtime, and interfaces with adjacent operations. Distinguish nominal equipment speed from conforming throughput for the whole cell. In sewing and garment equipment, soft materials, stretch, stack thickness, positioning variation, and style changes can affect results; trials should represent the expected material and style range.",
      "**Process design and execution control.** Define FAT, SAT, or a pilot protocol covering functions, recipes, safety interlocks, output quality, fault recovery, retained data, spare parts, and maintenance capability. Run trials under actual conditions against agreed criteria. Record software version, tooling, jigs, materials, operators, and run duration. Train against a competency matrix covering routine operation, setup/changeover, first-level troubleshooting, maintenance, lockout/tagout, and escalation. Control revisions and approvals for recipe or configuration changes.",
      "**Verification, deviation handling, and records.** Evaluate good output, first-pass yield, actual cycle time, changeover, downtime, staffing, WIP, ergonomics, and maintenance cost after implementation. Compare equivalent product mixes or normalize differences. Classify stoppages by cause rather than using a generic “technical issue” label. Provide a safe manual fallback with quality controls if the system fails. Expand only after stable pilot results across shifts, operators, and representative product groups."
    ],
    "deep": "Human factors include reach, posture, lifting loads, warning signals, visibility, and attention. Automation may shift workers from repetitive tasks to monitoring several machines, increasing cognitive load. Design clear fault messages, convenient material presentation, and reasonable intervention times. Collect feedback from operators.\n\n**Business analysis and scope.** For “Human Factors and Human–Machine Work Design,” establish the current process before investing: inputs, outputs, product variants, operator tasks, cycle time, changeover time, defects, downtime, and interfaces with adjacent operations. Distinguish nominal equipment speed from conforming throughput for the whole cell. In sewing and garment equipment, soft materials, stretch, stack thickness, positioning variation, and style changes can affect results; trials should represent the expected material and style range.\n\n**Process design and execution control.** Define FAT, SAT, or a pilot protocol covering functions, recipes, safety interlocks, output quality, fault recovery, retained data, spare parts, and maintenance capability. Run trials under actual conditions against agreed criteria. Record software version, tooling, jigs, materials, operators, and run duration. Train against a competency matrix covering routine operation, setup/changeover, first-level troubleshooting, maintenance, lockout/tagout, and escalation. Control revisions and approvals for recipe or configuration changes.\n\n**Verification, deviation handling, and records.** Evaluate good output, first-pass yield, actual cycle time, changeover, downtime, staffing, WIP, ergonomics, and maintenance cost after implementation. Compare equivalent product mixes or normalize differences. Classify stoppages by cause rather than using a generic “technical issue” label. Provide a safe manual fallback with quality controls if the system fails. Expand only after stable pilot results across shifts, operators, and representative product groups.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Select a specific garment order with a style code, PO, quantity, colors/sizes, and delivery milestones. Apply “Human Factors and Human–Machine Work Design” to prepare a decision record. Identify the input data and revisions, assumptions, approvers, timeline, risks, evidence to collect, and closure criteria. Create a realistic deviation scenario and distinguish containment, root cause, and preventive action. Do not invent legal limits or acceptance criteria when the applicable specification or contractual clause has not been established.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase18-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Human Factors and Human–Machine Work Design\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Human Factors and Human–Machine Work Design</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Human Factors and Human–Machine Work Design</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technology implementation flow and verification points</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Bottleneck</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">analysis</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Human–machine</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">work design</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pilot</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Line balancing</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Measure the baseline and verify under actual production conditions.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "Digital Skills Training and Competency Matrices for Technicians",
    "cat": "People and Change",
    "body": [
      "Automated factories require skills in mechanics, electrical systems, pneumatics, PLCs, sensors, machine vision, networks, data, and machine safety. Technicians also need to understand garment processes so they can distinguish machine faults from material or handling problems.",
      "Build a competency matrix with levels for basic operation, common fault recovery, preventive maintenance, advanced diagnosis, and programming. Restrict program-editing rights to trained and authorized personnel.",
      "Assess competence through practical equipment tasks, checklists, and fault scenarios. Update the matrix whenever machines, software, or processes change.",
      "**Business analysis and scope.** For “Digital Skills Training and Competency Matrices for Technicians,” establish the current process before investing: inputs, outputs, product variants, operator tasks, cycle time, changeover time, defects, downtime, and interfaces with adjacent operations. Distinguish nominal equipment speed from conforming throughput for the whole cell. With sewing and garment equipment, soft materials, stretch, stack thickness, positioning variation, and style changes affect performance; training and validation should reflect the actual equipment and material range.",
      "**Process design and execution control.** Define FAT, SAT, or a pilot protocol for functions, recipes, safety interlocks, output quality, fault recovery, retained data, spare parts, and maintenance capability. Run trials under actual conditions against agreed criteria, recording software versions, tooling, jigs, materials, operators, and run duration. Use a competency matrix for routine operation, setup/changeover, first-level troubleshooting, maintenance, lockout/tagout, and escalation. Control revisions and approval rights for recipe and configuration changes.",
      "**Verification, deviation handling, and records.** Assess good output, first-pass yield, actual cycle time, changeover, downtime, staffing, WIP, ergonomics, and maintenance cost after implementation. Compare equivalent product mixes or normalize differences. Classify stoppages by cause and provide a safe, quality-controlled manual fallback. Expand only after stable results across shifts, operators, and representative product groups. Retain practical assessment evidence and authorization records."
    ],
    "deep": "Technicians need practical competence in mechanics, electrical systems, pneumatics, PLCs, sensors, networks, and equipment fault analysis. A skills matrix should record demonstrated level, assessor, and evidence of practical work. Train with real-machine scenarios, energy isolation, and recovery procedures. Update the matrix when machine models or software change.\n\n**Business analysis and scope.** For “Digital Skills Training and Competency Matrices for Technicians,” establish the current process before investing: inputs, outputs, product variants, operator tasks, cycle time, changeover time, defects, downtime, and interfaces with adjacent operations. Distinguish nominal equipment speed from conforming throughput for the whole cell. With sewing and garment equipment, soft materials, stretch, stack thickness, positioning variation, and style changes affect performance; training and validation should reflect the actual equipment and material range.\n\n**Process design and execution control.** Define FAT, SAT, or a pilot protocol for functions, recipes, safety interlocks, output quality, fault recovery, retained data, spare parts, and maintenance capability. Run trials under actual conditions against agreed criteria, recording software versions, tooling, jigs, materials, operators, and run duration. Use a competency matrix for routine operation, setup/changeover, first-level troubleshooting, maintenance, lockout/tagout, and escalation. Control revisions and approval rights for recipe and configuration changes.\n\n**Verification, deviation handling, and records.** Assess good output, first-pass yield, actual cycle time, changeover, downtime, staffing, WIP, ergonomics, and maintenance cost after implementation. Compare equivalent product mixes or normalize differences. Classify stoppages by cause and provide a safe, quality-controlled manual fallback. Expand only after stable results across shifts, operators, and representative product groups. Retain practical assessment evidence and authorization records.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Select a specific garment order with a style code, PO, quantity, colors/sizes, and delivery milestones. Apply “Digital Skills Training and Competency Matrices for Technicians” to prepare a decision record. Identify the input data and revisions, assumptions, approvers, timeline, risks, evidence to collect, and closure criteria. Create a realistic deviation scenario and distinguish containment, root cause, and preventive action. Do not invent legal limits or acceptance criteria when the applicable specification or contractual clause has not been established.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase18-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Digital Skills Training and Competency Matrices for Technicians\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Digital Skills Training and Competency Matrices for Technicians</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Digital Skills Training and Competency Matrices for Technicians</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technology implementation flow and verification points</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Current skills</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Training</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Transition</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Assessment</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Measure the baseline and verify under actual production conditions.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "Technology Implementation Change Management",
    "cat": "People and Change",
    "body": [
      "Introducing new equipment affects staffing, tasks, work standards, maintenance, quality, and production planning. Late communication can lead to resistance or incorrect machine use.",
      "Prepare a stakeholder map, communication and training plan, line-side support, and feedback process. Clarify changes in duties and responsibilities, while complying with labor law and relevant agreements.",
      "Set acceptance criteria for technical performance, safety, quality, productivity, and operator readiness. Assign an owner for the period after handover.",
      "**Business analysis and scope.** For “Technology Implementation Change Management,” identify affected stakeholders, changed tasks, required skills, and the transition date. Separate confirmed requirements from assumptions. Map how the new technology affects staffing, work instructions, maintenance, quality controls, production planning, and interfaces with other departments. Clarify which decisions require management, engineering, safety, quality, or worker-representative approval.",
      "**Process design and execution control.** Prepare a stakeholder map, communication schedule, role and responsibility matrix, training plan, line-side support, and issue-escalation route. Pilot the revised work method in a controlled area. Explain what changes, why they are necessary, how performance will be assessed, and where employees can raise concerns. Update standard operating procedures, maintenance responsibilities, and access permissions before the new process becomes routine.",
      "**Verification, deviation handling, and records.** Monitor technical performance, quality, productivity, safety observations, training completion, operator feedback, and actual use after launch. Compare results with the baseline and record unintended effects. If adoption is low or errors increase, distinguish unclear instructions, inadequate training, poor interface design, equipment limitations, and workload issues. Retain approvals, communication records, training evidence, action owners, and post-implementation reviews."
    ],
    "deep": "Change management should identify affected people, changed tasks, additional skills, and the transition schedule. Pilot on a limited scope, collect feedback, revise instructions, and confirm competence before expansion. Monitor productivity, defects, and actual use after launch. Explain the reason for the change and provide a clear support channel.\n\n**Business analysis and scope.** For “Technology Implementation Change Management,” identify affected stakeholders, changed tasks, required skills, and the transition date. Separate confirmed requirements from assumptions. Map how the new technology affects staffing, work instructions, maintenance, quality controls, production planning, and interfaces with other departments. Clarify which decisions require management, engineering, safety, quality, or worker-representative approval.\n\n**Process design and execution control.** Prepare a stakeholder map, communication schedule, role and responsibility matrix, training plan, line-side support, and issue-escalation route. Pilot the revised work method in a controlled area. Explain what changes, why they are necessary, how performance will be assessed, and where employees can raise concerns. Update standard operating procedures, maintenance responsibilities, and access permissions before the new process becomes routine.\n\n**Verification, deviation handling, and records.** Monitor technical performance, quality, productivity, safety observations, training completion, operator feedback, and actual use after launch. Compare results with the baseline and record unintended effects. If adoption is low or errors increase, distinguish unclear instructions, inadequate training, poor interface design, equipment limitations, and workload issues. Retain approvals, communication records, training evidence, action owners, and post-implementation reviews.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Select a specific garment order with a style code, PO, quantity, colors/sizes, and delivery milestones. Apply “Technology Implementation Change Management” to prepare a decision record. Identify the input data and revisions, assumptions, approvers, timeline, risks, evidence to collect, and closure criteria. Create a realistic deviation scenario and distinguish containment, root cause, and preventive action. Do not invent legal limits or acceptance criteria when the applicable specification or contractual clause has not been established.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase18-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Technology Implementation Change Management\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Technology Implementation Change Management</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Technology Implementation Change Management</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technology implementation flow and verification points</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Current skills</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Training</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Transition</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Assessment</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Measure the baseline and verify under actual production conditions.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "FAT, SAT, and Machinery Acceptance",
    "cat": "Project Governance",
    "body": [
      "Factory Acceptance Testing (FAT) is performed at the supplier's site before delivery; Site Acceptance Testing (SAT) is performed after installation at the factory. Their scope depends on the contract and equipment type.",
      "An acceptance protocol should define material samples, products, speed, quality, safety, accessories, documentation, training, and environmental conditions. Acceptance should not be based solely on whether the machine powers up and runs.",
      "Record each defect, severity, responsible owner, correction deadline, and closure condition. Payment and production release must follow the contract and required approvals.",
      "**Business analysis and scope.** For “FAT, SAT, and Machinery Acceptance,” establish the current process before investment: inputs, outputs, product variants, operator tasks, cycle time, changeover time, defects, downtime, and interfaces with adjacent operations. Distinguish nominal equipment speed from conforming throughput for the entire cell. With sewing and garment equipment, soft materials, stretch, stack thickness, positioning variation, and style changes affect performance; test samples should represent the expected material and style range.",
      "**Process design and execution control.** Define FAT, SAT, or a pilot protocol covering functions, recipes, safety interlocks, output quality, fault recovery, retained data, spare parts, and maintenance capability. Run trials under actual conditions against agreed criteria. Record software version, tooling, jigs, materials, operators, and run duration. Train against a competency matrix for routine operation, setup/changeover, first-level troubleshooting, maintenance, lockout/tagout, and escalation. Control revisions and approvals for recipe or configuration changes.",
      "**Verification, deviation handling, and records.** Evaluate good output, first-pass yield, actual cycle time, changeover, downtime, staffing, WIP, ergonomics, and maintenance cost. Compare equivalent product mixes or normalize differences. Classify stoppages by cause and document every deviation with its severity, owner, deadline, and closure evidence. Use a safe manual fallback if the system fails. Sign acceptance only when the agreed criteria are supported by evidence and contractual approvals are complete."
    ],
    "deep": "FAT verifies the machine against requirements at the supplier's site; SAT verifies it after installation under factory conditions. Prepare a protocol covering functions, cycle time, safety, output quality, data interfaces, and documentation. Record deviations, severity, owner, and closure conditions. Sign acceptance only when evidence meets the agreed criteria.\n\n**Business analysis and scope.** For “FAT, SAT, and Machinery Acceptance,” establish the current process before investment: inputs, outputs, product variants, operator tasks, cycle time, changeover time, defects, downtime, and interfaces with adjacent operations. Distinguish nominal equipment speed from conforming throughput for the entire cell. With sewing and garment equipment, soft materials, stretch, stack thickness, positioning variation, and style changes affect performance; test samples should represent the expected material and style range.\n\n**Process design and execution control.** Define FAT, SAT, or a pilot protocol covering functions, recipes, safety interlocks, output quality, fault recovery, retained data, spare parts, and maintenance capability. Run trials under actual conditions against agreed criteria. Record software version, tooling, jigs, materials, operators, and run duration. Train against a competency matrix for routine operation, setup/changeover, first-level troubleshooting, maintenance, lockout/tagout, and escalation. Control revisions and approvals for recipe or configuration changes.\n\n**Verification, deviation handling, and records.** Evaluate good output, first-pass yield, actual cycle time, changeover, downtime, staffing, WIP, ergonomics, and maintenance cost. Compare equivalent product mixes or normalize differences. Classify stoppages by cause and document every deviation with its severity, owner, deadline, and closure evidence. Use a safe manual fallback if the system fails. Sign acceptance only when the agreed criteria are supported by evidence and contractual approvals are complete.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Select a specific garment order with a style code, PO, quantity, colors/sizes, and delivery milestones. Apply “FAT, SAT, and Machinery Acceptance” to prepare a decision record. Identify the input data and revisions, assumptions, approvers, timeline, risks, evidence to collect, and closure criteria. Create a realistic deviation scenario and distinguish containment, root cause, and preventive action. Do not invent legal limits or acceptance criteria when the applicable specification or contractual clause has not been established.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase18-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"FAT, SAT, and Machinery Acceptance\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>FAT, SAT, and Machinery Acceptance</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">FAT, SAT, and Machinery Acceptance</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technology implementation flow and verification points</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Requirements</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">FAT/SAT</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Acceptance</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Performance monitoring</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Measure the baseline and verify under actual production conditions.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "Spare-Parts and Equipment Lifecycle Management",
    "cat": "Project Management",
    "body": [
      "Critical spare parts should be classified by lead time, failure frequency, substitutability, and the consequences of equipment stoppage. Too little stock increases downtime; excessive stock ties up working capital and creates obsolescence risk.",
      "Record the spare-part number, machine model, revision, supplier, storage location, and compatible equipment. Control replacement parts whose specifications differ from the original components.",
      "Review the parts catalogue periodically using consumption history and equipment changes. Plan for end-of-life and identify replacement options.",
      "**Business analysis and scope.** For spare-parts and equipment lifecycle management, define the current process before making an investment: inputs, outputs, product variants, operator tasks, cycle time, changeover time, defects, downtime, and interfaces with upstream and downstream operations. Distinguish the machine's rated speed from conforming throughput at cell level. In garment equipment, soft materials, stretch, stack thickness, positioning variation, and style changes can affect results; trials must therefore represent the expected material and product range.",
      "**Process design and execution control.** Define FAT, SAT, or a pilot protocol covering functions, recipes, safety interlocks, output quality, fault recovery, retained data, spare parts, and maintenance capability. Run trials under actual conditions against pre-agreed criteria. Record software versions, tooling, jigs, materials, operators, and run time. Train against a competency matrix covering routine operation, setup and changeover, first-line troubleshooting, maintenance, lockout/tagout, and escalation. Manage revisions and approval rights for every recipe or configuration change.",
      "**Verification, deviation handling, and records.** Evaluate implementation using good output, first-pass yield, actual cycle time, changeover, downtime, staffing, WIP, ergonomics, and maintenance cost; compare equivalent product mixes or normalize the differences. Classify stoppages by cause code rather than grouping everything as a technical issue. Prepare a manual fallback that has been assessed for safety and quality control. Scale up only after the pilot demonstrates stability across shifts, operators, and representative product groups."
    ],
    "deep": "Manage spare parts according to criticality, lead time, consumption, and equipment-stoppage risk. Distinguish safety-related parts, wear parts, and electronic components at risk of obsolescence. Record serial numbers, firmware, compatibility, and storage locations. Plan replacements when equipment support expires or the supplier discontinues production.\n\n**Business analysis and scope.** For spare-parts and equipment lifecycle management, define the current process before making an investment: inputs, outputs, product variants, operator tasks, cycle time, changeover time, defects, downtime, and interfaces with upstream and downstream operations. Distinguish the machine's rated speed from conforming throughput at cell level. In garment equipment, soft materials, stretch, stack thickness, positioning variation, and style changes can affect results; trials must therefore represent the expected material and product range.\n\n**Process design and execution control.** Define FAT, SAT, or a pilot protocol covering functions, recipes, safety interlocks, output quality, fault recovery, retained data, spare parts, and maintenance capability. Run trials under actual conditions against pre-agreed criteria. Record software versions, tooling, jigs, materials, operators, and run time. Train against a competency matrix covering routine operation, setup and changeover, first-line troubleshooting, maintenance, lockout/tagout, and escalation. Manage revisions and approval rights for every recipe or configuration change.\n\n**Verification, deviation handling, and records.** Evaluate implementation using good output, first-pass yield, actual cycle time, changeover, downtime, staffing, WIP, ergonomics, and maintenance cost; compare equivalent product mixes or normalize the differences. Classify stoppages by cause code rather than grouping everything as a technical issue. Prepare a manual fallback that has been assessed for safety and quality control. Scale up only after the pilot demonstrates stability across shifts, operators, and representative product groups.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Select a specific garment order with a style code, PO, quantity, color/size, and delivery milestones. Apply spare-parts and equipment lifecycle management to prepare a decision record. Specify input data and revisions, assumptions, approvers, dates, risks, evidence to collect, and closure criteria. Create a realistic deviation scenario and distinguish containment, root cause, and preventive action. Do not invent legal limits or acceptance criteria where no applicable specification or contractual clause has been provided.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase18-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Spare-Parts and Equipment Lifecycle Management\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Spare-Parts and Equipment Lifecycle Management</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Spare-Parts and Equipment Lifecycle Management</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technology implementation and verification flow</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Requirements</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">FAT/SAT</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Acceptance</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Performance monitoring</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Measure the baseline and verify under actual production conditions.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "Measuring the Results of an Automation Investment",
    "cat": "Project Management",
    "body": [
      "Before-and-after comparisons must use consistent definitions and the same product mix, or adjust for mix differences. Track output, quality, downtime, labor, WIP, lead time, and operating cost.",
      "Separate realized benefits from projected benefits. If output increases because of overtime or additional workers, do not attribute the entire gain to the new machine.",
      "Conduct a review after 30, 60, and 90 days, or at an appropriate interval. Record lessons learned, integration issues, and the conditions required before scaling up.",
      "**Business analysis and scope.** For post-investment automation performance measurement, define the current process before investment: inputs, outputs, product variants, operator tasks, cycle time, changeover time, defects, downtime, and interfaces with upstream and downstream operations. Distinguish rated machine speed from conforming cell throughput. In garment equipment, soft materials, stretch, stack thickness, positioning variation, and style changes can affect results; trials must represent the expected material and product range.",
      "**Process design and execution control.** Define FAT, SAT, or a pilot protocol covering functions, recipes, safety interlocks, output quality, fault recovery, retained data, spare parts, and maintenance capability. Run trials under actual conditions against pre-agreed criteria. Record software versions, tooling, jigs, materials, operators, and run time. Train against a competency matrix covering routine operation, setup and changeover, first-line troubleshooting, maintenance, lockout/tagout, and escalation. Manage revisions and approval rights for every recipe or configuration change.",
      "**Verification, deviation handling, and records.** Evaluate implementation using good output, first-pass yield, actual cycle time, changeover, downtime, staffing, WIP, ergonomics, and maintenance cost; compare equivalent product mixes or normalize the differences. Classify stoppages by cause code rather than grouping everything as a technical issue. Prepare a manual fallback that has been assessed for safety and quality control. Scale up only after the pilot demonstrates stability across shifts, operators, and representative product groups."
    ],
    "deep": "Compare post-investment results with the baseline using consistent definitions, product mix, and observation periods. Measure output, quality, downtime, labor, consumables, energy, and maintenance cost. Calculate actual operating costs and capacity utilization. Record benefits that were not achieved, their causes, corrective actions, and review deadlines.\n\n**Business analysis and scope.** For post-investment automation performance measurement, define the current process before investment: inputs, outputs, product variants, operator tasks, cycle time, changeover time, defects, downtime, and interfaces with upstream and downstream operations. Distinguish rated machine speed from conforming cell throughput. In garment equipment, soft materials, stretch, stack thickness, positioning variation, and style changes can affect results; trials must represent the expected material and product range.\n\n**Process design and execution control.** Define FAT, SAT, or a pilot protocol covering functions, recipes, safety interlocks, output quality, fault recovery, retained data, spare parts, and maintenance capability. Run trials under actual conditions against pre-agreed criteria. Record software versions, tooling, jigs, materials, operators, and run time. Train against a competency matrix covering routine operation, setup and changeover, first-line troubleshooting, maintenance, lockout/tagout, and escalation. Manage revisions and approval rights for every recipe or configuration change.\n\n**Verification, deviation handling, and records.** Evaluate implementation using good output, first-pass yield, actual cycle time, changeover, downtime, staffing, WIP, ergonomics, and maintenance cost; compare equivalent product mixes or normalize the differences. Classify stoppages by cause code rather than grouping everything as a technical issue. Prepare a manual fallback that has been assessed for safety and quality control. Scale up only after the pilot demonstrates stability across shifts, operators, and representative product groups.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Select a specific garment order with a style code, PO, quantity, color/size, and delivery milestones. Apply post-investment automation performance measurement to prepare a decision record. Specify input data and revisions, assumptions, approvers, dates, risks, evidence to collect, and closure criteria. Create a realistic deviation scenario and distinguish containment, root cause, and preventive action. Do not invent legal limits or acceptance criteria where no applicable specification or contractual clause has been provided.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase18-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Measuring the Results of an Automation Investment\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Measuring the Results of an Automation Investment</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Measuring the Results of an Automation Investment</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technology implementation and verification flow</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Requirements</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">FAT/SAT</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Acceptance</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Performance monitoring</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Measure the baseline and verify under actual production conditions.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "Case Study: Evaluating an Automated Cutting Cell Investment Using Baseline Data",
    "cat": "Implementation Case Studies",
    "body": [
      "Scenario: A cutting room handles four product styles, frequently changes marker layouts, and experiences waiting time between spreading, cutting, and bundle numbering. A supplier proposes an automatic spreading machine and cutter. Before comparing quotations, the project team must measure a baseline using consistent units: fabric input in meters, number of plies, conforming cut components, preparation time, machine run time, waiting time, labor, and cutting defects.",
      "Illustrative data for one shift: 420 working minutes; 55 minutes waiting for materials or markers; 35 minutes for setup; 250 minutes of machine operation; 40 minutes handling faults; and 40 minutes for other activities. Reconcile the total against actual observations and classify time consistently to avoid double-counting downtime. If the new machine only shortens cutting time while the constraint is marker approval or fabric supply, total cutting-room output may not increase proportionally.",
      "Build a business case covering capital cost, installation, electricity and compressed air, software, training, maintenance, blades and consumables, spare parts, downtime, and cost of capital. Separate the benefits: genuinely reduced labor hours, measurable material savings, less recutting, better schedule responsiveness, and improved traceability. Do not convert saved time into monetary savings unless an approved capacity-use plan or cost reduction supports it.",
      "Run the pilot on a representative group of styles. Record marker version, fabric type, ply count, lay stability, component accuracy, setup time, edge defects, and stoppage time. Compare before and after using an equivalent product mix or explain the differences. Agree on acceptance conditions before FAT/SAT.",
      "Exercise: identify three missing data points before calculating payback; identify the operation that could become the bottleneck after investment; and propose conditions for stopping the pilot. The figures above are illustrative training data, not a standard cutting-machine productivity benchmark."
    ],
    "deep": "For a cutting-cell investment, establish a baseline by fabric type, ply count, number of components, marker efficiency, setup time, cutting time, labor, and defects. Compare automation options across several order mixes rather than one favorable style. Include fixtures, maintenance, blades, software, and downtime in the cost model. Pilot with a representative production lot and verify bundle quality.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase18-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Case Study: Evaluating an Automated Cutting Cell Investment Using Baseline Data\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Case Study: Evaluating an Automated Cutting Cell Investment Using Baseline Data</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Case Study: Evaluating an Automated Cutting Cell Investment Using Baseline Data</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technology implementation and verification flow</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Baseline</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pilot design</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Verification</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Measure the baseline and verify under actual production conditions.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "Case Study: Validating a Vision System for Fabric Defect Inspection",
    "cat": "Implementation Case Studies",
    "body": [
      "Scenario: A factory wants to use cameras to detect fabric surface defects before spreading. First define the defect scope: holes, stains, streaks, weaving faults, shade variation, or finishing defects. A single label such as “fabric defect” is inadequate when definitions, sizes, and consequences differ.",
      "Build a traceable sample dataset containing fabric roll, lot, color, fabric face, running speed, lighting, defect location, and inspector disposition. Separate development data from an independent validation set. Avoid placing near-duplicate images from the same roll in both sets, as this can make evaluation results overly optimistic. Record cases where inspectors disagree.",
      "Evaluate each defect class with a confusion matrix: true positives, false positives, false negatives, and true negatives. Precision asks how many alerts are correct; recall asks how many actual defects the system detects. For critical defects, a missed detection may have greater consequences than an extra alert. Set thresholds using risk assessment and the response procedure.",
      "Test under real production conditions, including changes in color, texture, gloss, speed, vibration, dust, lighting, and roll joints. Require human confirmation when the system is uncertain. Retain images, locations, roll IDs, model versions, and final decisions. Define what to do when the camera is dirty, connectivity is lost, or the model is unavailable.",
      "Do not release the system based only on an overall accuracy percentage. Results are needed by defect type, severity, fabric type, and operating condition. Define who may change the model and how it will be revalidated after changes to the camera, lighting, or configuration. Exercise: choose the priority metric for a critical defect and explain the trade-off with false alarms."
    ],
    "deep": "Vision inspection requires a defined defect taxonomy, observation area, minimum defect size, and disposition after detection. Capture representative images across colors, patterns, gloss levels, and lighting conditions. Separate training and evaluation sets; record false accepts and false rejects by defect type. Define how operators confirm results, relabel samples, and handle model drift.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase18-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Case Study: Validating a Vision System for Fabric Defect Inspection\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Case Study: Validating a Vision System for Fabric Defect Inspection</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Case Study: Validating a Vision System for Fabric Defect Inspection</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technology implementation and verification flow</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Baseline</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pilot design</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Verification</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Measure the baseline and verify under actual production conditions.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "Case Study: Piloting RFID/MES Traceability for Work in Progress on a Sewing Line",
    "cat": "Implementation Case Studies",
    "body": [
      "Scenario: On a multi-operation sewing line, workers spend time locating bundles and cannot easily identify which operation currently holds work in progress. An RFID/MES pilot is proposed for one line before wider deployment. Before implementation, map the physical flow from cutting and bundling through line feeding, each operation, final inspection, and packing. Identify where identification is needed and which events have real management value.",
      "Design consistent identifiers for style, PO, color, size, bundle, operation, line, and revision. Do not confuse a bundle ID with an individual garment ID. Each event should contain a timestamp, read point, object ID, status, and event source. Define how to handle duplicate reads, incorrect reads, missing tags, bundle changes, bundle splitting, and data corrections.",
      "Initially run the new system in parallel with the existing method. Reconcile input and output quantities, WIP by operation, items sent to the wrong line, missing events, and inventory discrepancies. RFID can read quickly, but metal, liquids, tag placement, and tag density can affect read performance. Survey the actual site rather than assuming the read zone is always reliable.",
      "The dashboard must use consistent definitions: WIP is the quantity currently in the system under a defined status; throughput is the quantity completed during a specified period; lead time is the elapsed time between agreed start and end points. Do not mix scan events with actual production output if scans can be skipped or entered late.",
      "Before scaling up, finalize data-edit permissions, audit trails, network-outage procedures, time synchronization, backups, and reconciliation responsibilities. Exercise: design a minimum event record and identify three situations in which the dashboard could be wrong even though the tag reader is working."
    ],
    "deep": "An RFID/MES pilot needs a unique identifier for each bundle or work-in-progress unit and clearly defined event points along the line. Test reading distance, orientation, and tag density under actual conditions; check for duplicate, missed, and incorrectly assigned reads. Reconcile system data with physical counts by shift. Design a manual-entry fallback for reader or network failures.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase18-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Case Study: Piloting RFID/MES Traceability for Work in Progress on a Sewing Line\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Case Study: Piloting RFID/MES Traceability for Work in Progress on a Sewing Line</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Case Study: Piloting RFID/MES Traceability for Work in Progress on a Sewing Line</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technology implementation and verification flow</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Baseline</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pilot design</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Verification</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Measure the baseline and verify under actual production conditions.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "Case Study: Predictive Maintenance for Sewing and Cutting Machines",
    "cat": "Implementation Case Studies",
    "body": [
      "Scenario: A factory operates many sewing machines and several CNC cutters; unexpected stoppages disrupt the production plan. The maintenance team wants to introduce condition monitoring. Before buying sensors, list equipment-specific failure modes: bearings, motors, drives, pneumatics, vacuum systems, cutting blades, feeding mechanisms, and control faults.",
      "Distinguish condition-monitoring data from routine maintenance records. Temperature, vibration, current, pressure, cycle time, and alarm codes are meaningful only when linked to machine model, operating state, load, speed, environment, and repair history. An increasing value does not automatically prove that a failure is imminent; understand the physical relationship and confirm it through technical inspection.",
      "Choose a pilot group with a sufficiently clear stoppage history and suitable sensor options. Capture a baseline under normal operating modes. Record alert time, physical inspection findings, replaced components, confirmed cause, and post-intervention results. Track both false alarms and failures that were not detected so the logic can be adjusted.",
      "Do not replace safety maintenance or mandatory service schedules simply because a dashboard is available. Every alert must trigger a defined process: who receives it, who verifies it, when the machine is stopped, who authorizes intervention, and how records are retained. Avoid connecting sensors or software in ways that alter safety-control circuits.",
      "Evaluate the pilot using unplanned downtime, detection time, repair time, spare-parts cost, and alert reliability. Do not claim downtime reduction if pre- and post-periods differ in load, shifts, or output without adjustment. Exercise: select two suitable failure modes to monitor and identify the data required before building a model."
    ],
    "deep": "Select predictive-maintenance equipment where data are available and stoppage consequences are significant. Record vibration, temperature, and current baselines, fault history, and maintenance work; identify warning signs that can be inspected. Begin with alerts that support technicians rather than automatic component replacement. Measure alert accuracy, false alarms, and downtime avoided.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase18-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Case Study: Predictive Maintenance for Sewing and Cutting Machines\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Case Study: Predictive Maintenance for Sewing and Cutting Machines</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Case Study: Predictive Maintenance for Sewing and Cutting Machines</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technology implementation and verification flow</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Baseline</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pilot design</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Verification</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Measure the baseline and verify under actual production conditions.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "Case Study: Responding to an OT Network Incident and Restoring Operations",
    "cat": "Implementation Case Studies",
    "body": [
      "Scenario: A programmable sewing machine and an MES terminal lose connectivity after an unknown device is plugged into a network port in the production area. The operations team must maintain safety and contain the impact. Do not restart equipment, connect or disconnect devices, or change configurations outside the factory's incident-response procedure.",
      "Distinguish the OT zones that control machines from office IT systems, application servers, and remote-access devices. Create an asset and connection map covering machines, PLCs, HMIs, gateways, switches, servers, engineering accounts, supplier connections, and data flows. Record ownership, versions, criticality, and support arrangements for connectivity loss.",
      "The response procedure needs an incident lead, reporting method, approved isolation criteria, log and evidence preservation, supplier contacts, production and quality impact assessment, and restoration conditions. Direct actions on control equipment must be performed by authorized personnel in accordance with system documentation.",
      "Prepare backups of PLC/HMI configurations, machine programs, recipes, process parameters, and network configurations in advance; test restoration in an appropriate environment. A backup that has never been restore-tested does not prove recoverability. Control versions and approvals, and retain separate copies under the security policy.",
      "After the incident, verify correct machine operation, effective interlocks and safety functions, accurate production data, inspection of the first product, and reviewed access rights. Exercise: draw the connection flow and write a restoration checklist to be completed before the machine returns to production."
    ],
    "deep": "OT network response must prioritize personnel safety and machine state. Isolate the affected zone under the approved procedure, preserve log evidence, and identify which systems remain trusted. Restore from verified configuration backups, then check communications and interlocks before restarting. Record the cause, impact scope, and preventive actions.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase18-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Case Study: Responding to an OT Network Incident and Restoring Operations\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Case Study: Responding to an OT Network Incident and Restoring Operations</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Case Study: Responding to an OT Network Incident and Restoring Operations</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technology implementation and verification flow</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Baseline</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pilot design</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Verification</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Measure the baseline and verify under actual production conditions.</text></svg></figure>"
  }
